package teyru

/* java.util.concurrent's executor half: Callable, Future, ExecutorService, and
 * the pools Executors' factories hand out.
 *
 * The shape is Java's. A Future is the handle on a task; a worker thread takes
 * a task at a time off a queue and runs it on itself, so a task's stack,
 * allocations and monitors belong to that thread exactly as they would if the
 * program had started the Thread itself; submit(Callable) answers a Future
 * whose get() waits for the answer; shutdown() stops the pool taking work and
 * lets the tasks that were already taken finish; awaitTermination waits for the
 * workers to leave.
 *
 * What this is built on, because it changes how a reader should reason about
 * it:
 *
 *   - Monitors, and only monitors, for everything but the waiting. The queue is
 *     an ArrayDeque under the pool's monitor, a Future's answer is written under
 *     its own, and AtomicInteger in 38_concurrent_collections is a monitor.
 *     The runtime has no hardware atomics, so nothing in this file is lock-free
 *     and nothing scales the way java.util.concurrent scales: a pool of four
 *     threads taking tasks from one queue contends on one monitor, and that is
 *     the price of the simple correct thing.
 *
 *   - THE POOL AND THE FUTURES DO NOT USE Object.wait. They poll instead: a
 *     worker with nothing to do, and a get() whose answer is not there yet,
 *     sleep for Concurrent.POLL_MILLIS and look again. This is not what
 *     java.util.concurrent does -- a Java worker parks on a condition variable
 *     and a task wakes it -- and the reason is not taste, it is that this
 *     runtime's monitor waits are broken:
 *
 *       - four threads inside Object.wait on one object, woken by notifyAll,
 *         lose the wakeup about one run in four (a program that waits this way
 *         hangs), and sometimes throw IllegalMonitorStateException out of the
 *         wait that follows;
 *       - and even one thread waiting on a monitor of its own eventually
 *         corrupts that monitor's ownership: a worker parked on its own signal
 *         has been seen to throw IllegalMonitorStateException: "current thread
 *         is not owner" from the very next synchronized block it left, with a
 *         plain-language pool of four workers failing this way in every run of
 *         a test that ran a few hundred tasks.
 *
 *     Both were reproduced with programs that use nothing but the prelude --
 *     Thread, synchronized, Object.wait/notifyAll -- so this is the runtime,
 *     not this file. What this file does about it is wait on nothing when it
 *     can poll instead: Thread.sleep takes no monitor, so a pooling worker
 *     cannot corrupt one. The price is honest and worth stating: a task
 *     submitted to an idle pool is picked up within Concurrent.POLL_MILLIS
 *     rather than immediately, and an idle worker wakes twelve times a second
 *     to look at its queue. For a pool of four that is nothing; for a pool of
 *     four hundred it would be 4800 wakeups a second, and a reader deciding how
 *     many workers to keep should know it.
 *
 *   - CountDownLatch, in 38_concurrent_collections.teyru, is the one place
 *     built on Object.wait/notifyAll, because that is what a synchronizer is.
 *     It waits the only way that is safe here: each waiting thread has a signal
 *     of its own (Signal, below), so no monitor ever holds two waiters, and the
 *     wait runs in slices of Concurrent.WAIT_SLICE_MILLIS and re-examines the
 *     count, so a notification the runtime loses costs one slice rather than a
 *     hang.
 *
 *   - There is no interrupt. Nothing in this language can stop a thread that is
 *     inside a task, so shutdownNow() cannot mean what it means in Java: it
 *     drops the tasks that have not started and hands them back, and the tasks
 *     that are running run to their end. Its comment says so, and no method
 *     here takes Java's mayInterruptIfRunning flag, because a flag that
 *     silently did nothing would mislead exactly the reader it was for.
 *
 *   - There is no TimeUnit in this standard library. Every duration here is
 *     milliseconds, the unit Thread.sleep takes.
 *
 * A task that throws is reported, never swallowed: FutureTask.run catches the
 * throwable and keeps it, and get() throws ExecutionException with it as the
 * cause, which is what Java does. A task that came in through execute() and
 * throws has no Future to carry it, and it is handled the way Java's worker
 * handles it: the worker thread dies with the throwable (the runtime's uncaught
 * handler prints Java's line), and the pool starts a replacement so a fixed
 * pool does not quietly shrink.
 *
 * A pool this program does not shut down outlives it: the runtime waits for
 * every thread the program started before it exits, so a program that builds a
 * pool and returns from main without shutting it down does not end until that
 * pool's workers do -- which for a fixed pool is never. Shut your pools down.
 *
 * Not implemented, and deliberately not declared: TimeUnit, invokeAll,
 * invokeAny, submit(Runnable), the scheduled executor, the fork/join pool,
 * CompletionService, ThreadFactory, the uncaught-exception handler, and
 * ThreadPoolExecutor's own tuning surface. */

/* The intervals this library's blocking waits are built from, in milliseconds.
 *
 * Two of them, because there are two kinds of wait here and they pay for
 * different things: see the file comment for why the pool polls and the latch
 * waits. */
class Concurrent {
  private Concurrent() {
  }

  /* How long a thread that polls sleeps between two looks at its condition.
     The pool's workers and Future.get() use this. Short enough that a task
     submitted to an idle pool starts promptly and a finished task is noticed
     promptly; long enough that a sleeping worker costs almost nothing. */
  public static final long POLL_MILLIS = 5L

  /* How long one Object.wait runs before the waiting thread looks at its
     condition again. CountDownLatch's waits use this. A notification that
     arrives is what ends the wait, as in Java; this is only the ceiling on how
     long a notification the runtime drops can delay a waiter before it notices
     the condition itself. */
  public static final long WAIT_SLICE_MILLIS = 25L
}

/* A place for exactly one thread to wait: the one kind of monitor wait this
   library is willing to do.
 *
 * A Signal is a monitor with one waiter and a flag: raise() sets the flag and
 * wakes the waiter, await() waits for the flag and lowers it. Because the flag
 * survives a raise that arrives before anybody waits, the two can be called in
 * either order without losing anything -- and because the flag, not the
 * notification, is what await() answers from, a notification this runtime drops
 * delays a waiter by one slice instead of stranding it.
 *
 * One thread awaits one Signal. That is the point of the class: several threads
 * inside Object.wait on the same object is the pattern this runtime does not
 * carry (see the file comment), and a Signal is how 38's CountDownLatch waits
 * without ever falling into it. */
class Signal {
  private boolean raised

  /* Raise it. Any thread may, as often as it likes, and it is not an error to
     raise a signal nobody is waiting for: the next await answers at once. */
  public void raise() {
    synchronized (this) {
      this.raised = true
      this.notifyAll()
    }
  }

  /* Wait until it is raised, then lower it. It waits in slices and looks at the
     flag again after each one, so a lost notification costs a slice, not a
     hang; the flag is checked under the monitor it was set under, so the look
     is never at a stale value. */
  public void await() {
    boolean up = this.await(Concurrent.WAIT_SLICE_MILLIS)
    while (!up) {
      up = this.await(Concurrent.WAIT_SLICE_MILLIS)
    }
  }

  /* Wait at most this many milliseconds: true when it was raised (and lowered),
     false when the time ran out. A false answer lowers nothing, so a raise that
     comes later is still there for the next call. */
  public boolean await(long millis) {
    long deadline = System.nanoTime() + millis * 1000000L
    synchronized (this) {
      while (!this.raised) {
        long left = deadline - System.nanoTime()
        if (left <= 0) {
          return false
        }
        long waitMillis = left / 1000000L
        if (waitMillis <= 0) {
          /* under a millisecond left, and this language's wait counts in
             milliseconds: spend it as one, and let the loop answer */
          waitMillis = 1
        }
        this.wait(waitMillis)
      }
      this.raised = false
      return true
    }
  }
}

/* The waiting half of a condition that some other object owns: the list a
   CountDownLatch keeps of the signals its waiters are parked on.
 *
 * A thread that has to wait parks a Signal of its own here (under the latch's
 * monitor) and then waits on its own signal; the thread that empties the latch
 * wakes them all. Every method here is called with the latch's monitor held:
 * park and leave while the waiter is deciding, wakeAll once the count is zero.
 * Nothing in it takes a lock of its own, and no lock is ever taken in the other
 * order -- a waiter holds only its own signal while it waits. */
class Waiters {
  private ArrayList<Signal> parked = new ArrayList<Signal>()

  /* Take a signal that is about to wait. */
  public void park(Signal s) {
    this.parked.add(s)
  }

  /* Wake every parked waiter. */
  public void wakeAll() {
    for (Signal s : this.parked) {
      s.raise()
    }
    this.parked.clear()
  }

  /* Forget a waiter that gave up on its own clock. */
  public void leave(Signal s) {
    this.parked.remove(s)
  }
}

/* A task that answers a value. Java's functional interfaces cannot be written
   as lambdas here -- this language has none -- so a task is an object with a
   call method, which is what a program passes to submit. */
interface Callable<V> {
  V call()
}

/* The handle on a task: Java's Future, with Java's four methods. */
interface Future<V> {
  /* The answer, waiting for it until it is there. Throws ExecutionException
     carrying the task's own throwable when the task threw, and
     CancellationException when it was cancelled. */
  V get()
  /* True once the task has finished, thrown, or been cancelled. */
  boolean isDone()
  boolean isCancelled()
  /* Cancel a task that has not started yet: the worker that takes it will find
     it cancelled and skip it. A task that has already started is not cancelled
     and this answers false -- there is no interrupt in this language, so
     nothing could stop it, and Java's cancel(true) is the form that would
     promise one. Java's cancel(false), which is this. */
  boolean cancel()
}

/* Something that runs a Runnable. Java's Executor, and the half of the
   interface that has nothing to do with answers. */
interface Executor {
  void execute(Runnable command)
}

/* Java's ExecutorService: submitting, and shutting down. */
interface ExecutorService extends Executor {
  <T> Future<T> submit(Callable<T> task)
  /* Stop taking work. The tasks already handed to the pool -- the ones queued
     and the ones running -- finish, and then the workers leave. */
  void shutdown()
  /* Stop now: no new task is taken and the queued ones are handed back. It
     cannot stop a task that is already running, because nothing here can
     interrupt a thread; that task runs to its end. The futures of the tasks
     handed back never complete, as in Java. */
  List<Runnable> shutdownNow()
  /* Wait up to this many milliseconds for every worker to leave, answering
     true when the pool has finished. A duration of zero or less does not wait;
     it answers whether the pool has already finished. The wait is a poll of
     the worker count every Concurrent.POLL_MILLIS, because the pool's monitor
     has no waiters to notify. */
  boolean awaitTermination(long millis)
  boolean isShutdown()
  /* True once shutdown() or shutdownNow() has been called and every worker has
     left. A pool nobody has shut down is never terminated. */
  boolean isTerminated()
}

/* What a task's failure arrives as: get() throws this, with the throwable the
   task threw as its cause. Java's class of the same name, and checked there
   and here -- although this language has no throws clause, so nothing makes a
   caller write the catch. It is the reason a failed task is never silently
   answered with null. */
class ExecutionException extends Exception {
  public ExecutionException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
  public ExecutionException(String message, Throwable cause) {
    super(message, cause)
  }
  /* Java's Throwable(cause) copies the cause's toString into the message; this
     standard library's sets the cause and leaves the message null, so the
     copy is made here to keep the message a program prints the same one Java
     would. */
  public ExecutionException(Throwable cause) {
    super(cause.toString(), cause)
  }
}

/* get() on a future whose task was cancelled. Java's CancellationException,
   which extends IllegalStateException for the same reason: the program that
   cancelled the task is the program calling get(). */
class CancellationException extends IllegalStateException {
  public CancellationException() {
  }
  public CancellationException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

/* Handed to execute() or submit() after the pool has been shut down. Java's
   class of the same name. */
class RejectedExecutionException extends RuntimeException {
  public RejectedExecutionException() {
  }
  public RejectedExecutionException(String message) {
    super(message)
  }
}

/* A task with a place to put its answer: Java's FutureTask, which is both the
   Future a caller holds and the Runnable a worker runs.
 *
 * The answer, the throwable and the flags are written and read under this
 * object's monitor, so get() and run() agree about whether the answer is there;
 * get() polls that flag rather than waiting on the monitor, for the reason the
 * file comment gives. Any number of threads may call get() on one future: none
 * of them waits, so none of them shares anything but the monitor they read the
 * flag under. */
class FutureTask<T> implements Future<T>, Runnable {
  private Callable<T> callable
  private T value
  private Throwable failure
  /* done: the task has finished, thrown or been cancelled. running: a worker
     is inside call() right now, which is what makes cancel() refuse -- a
     cancelled task is one that never started. */
  private boolean done
  private boolean running
  private boolean cancelled

  public FutureTask(Callable<T> callable) {
    this.callable = callable
  }

  /* Run the task. A worker thread calls this; so may a program, and then the
     task runs on the calling thread. Running a task twice is a no-op the
     second time, as it is in Java. */
  public void run() {
    Callable<T> task
    synchronized (this) {
      if (this.done || this.running) {
        return
      }
      this.running = true
      task = this.callable
    }
    /* Outside the monitor: the task may take minutes, may take monitors of its
       own, and may call get() on a future of its own. Holding this future's
       monitor across it would make a concurrent get() and a concurrent
       cancel() -- both of which only want to read or set a flag -- wait for
       the task to finish. */
    T answer = null
    Throwable thrown = null
    try {
      answer = task.call()
    } catch (Throwable t) {
      thrown = t
    }
    synchronized (this) {
      this.running = false
      this.value = answer
      this.failure = thrown
      this.done = true
    }
  }

  public T get() {
    while (true) {
      synchronized (this) {
        if (this.done) {
          return this.answer()
        }
      }
      /* Not waiting on this object's monitor, and not on one of its own: a poll
         of the flag under the monitor is enough, and Thread.sleep takes no
         monitor at all. */
      Thread.sleep(Concurrent.POLL_MILLIS)
    }
  }

  /* The answer, or the throwable standing in for it. Called with this object's
     monitor held, and only when the task is done. */
  private T answer() {
    if (this.cancelled) {
      throw new CancellationException("the task was cancelled")
    }
    if (this.failure != null) {
      throw new ExecutionException(this.failure)
    }
    return this.value
  }

  public boolean isDone() {
    synchronized (this) {
      return this.done
    }
  }

  public boolean isCancelled() {
    synchronized (this) {
      return this.cancelled
    }
  }

  public boolean cancel() {
    synchronized (this) {
      if (this.done || this.running) {
        return false
      }
      this.cancelled = true
      this.done = true
      return true
    }
  }
}

/* The pool behind all three factories.
 *
 * Two numbers describe a pool: how many workers it keeps alive (core -- its
 * workers never give up), and how long an idle worker may wait for work before
 * giving up (keepAlive, in milliseconds, 0 meaning never). A fixed pool of four
 * is 4 and 0; the single-thread executor is 1 and 0; the cached pool is 0 and
 * 60000, so no worker is kept, one is started whenever a task arrives and none
 * is idle, and one that has been idle a minute leaves.
 *
 * Everything the pool decides is decided under this object's monitor: the
 * queue, the number of live workers, and how many are idle. A worker takes a
 * task, or counts itself idle, in the same critical section, so a task can
 * never fall into the gap between "there is nothing to do" and "this worker
 * goes idle": either the worker finds the task, or it has already said it is
 * idle when the task arrives, and then execute() either has an idle worker to
 * pick it up or starts one. Nobody ever waits on this monitor -- the workers
 * sleep and look, and awaitTermination sleeps and looks -- so there is nothing
 * to notify, and no waiter for this runtime's wait to corrupt. */
class ThreadPool implements ExecutorService {
  /* Oldest first, taken from the front. ArrayDeque rather than ArrayList
     because taking a task has to cost O(1): an ArrayList would shift every
     element of the queue on every take. */
  private ArrayDeque<Runnable> queue = new ArrayDeque<Runnable>()
  private int core
  private long keepAlive
  private int workers
  /* The workers that have found the queue empty. What execute() reads to
     decide whether a cached pool has to start another worker, and what the
     keep-alive below counts down against. */
  private int idle
  /* shutdown: no new task is taken. stopped: shutdownNow was called, so the
     queued tasks were dropped and a dead worker is not replaced. */
  private boolean shutdown
  private boolean stopped
  private int created
  private String prefix

  /* Called with this pool's monitor held. */
  private void startWorker() {
    this.workers = this.workers + 1
    this.created = this.created + 1
    final ThreadPool self = this
    /* The worker is this thread's whole life: take a task, run it here, and
       sleep until there is another. It is written as an object rather than a
       method because a thread runs a Runnable, and the loop must go on for as
       long as the pool wants the worker. */
    Thread t = new Thread(new Runnable() {
      public void run() {
        while (true) {
          Runnable task = self.claim()
          if (task == null) {
            return
          }
          try {
            task.run()
          } catch (Throwable failure) {
            /* A task that came in through execute() has no Future to carry its
               failure, and this is the moment Java's worker thread dies with
               it: the runtime's uncaught handler prints Java's line ("Exception
               in thread ..."), the thread ends, and the program carries on.
               Putting a replacement in its place before it dies is what
               ThreadPoolExecutor does, and it is why a fixed pool is still four
               threads afterwards. A task that came in through submit() never
               reaches here: FutureTask catches its throwable and keeps it for
               get(). */
            self.workerFailed()
            throw failure
          }
        }
      }
    }, this.prefix + this.created)
    t.start()
  }

  public ThreadPool(int core, long keepAliveMillis, String prefix) {
    this.core = core
    this.keepAlive = keepAliveMillis
    this.prefix = prefix
    /* Under the monitor, which is what startWorker expects: a worker started
       a moment ago is already in claim(), and the fields it guards must not be
       written from outside the critical section it reads them in. The worker
       that has just been created blocks on this monitor until the constructor
       is done with it, which costs nothing. */
    synchronized (this) {
      for (int i = 0 : i < core : i++) {
        this.startWorker()
      }
    }
  }

  public void execute(Runnable command) {
    if (command == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("the task is null")
    }
    synchronized (this) {
      if (this.shutdown) {
        throw new RejectedExecutionException("the pool has been shut down")
      }
      this.queue.addLast(command)
      if (this.core == 0 && this.idle == 0) {
        /* no worker is kept, and none is sitting idle to take it: this is where
           a cached pool grows with the work offered to it */
        this.startWorker()
      }
    }
  }

  public <T> Future<T> submit(Callable<T> task) {
    if (task == null) {
      throw new NullPointerException("the task is null")
    }
    FutureTask<T> future = new FutureTask<T>(task)
    this.execute(future)
    return future
  }

  public void shutdown() {
    synchronized (this) {
      this.shutdown = true
    }
  }

  public List<Runnable> shutdownNow() {
    List<Runnable> dropped = new ArrayList<Runnable>()
    synchronized (this) {
      /* What this cannot do is stop a task that has started: there is no
         interrupt in this language, so the worker that is inside run() is
         inside it until run() returns. What it does do is refuse new work,
         hand back every task that never started, and let the workers leave as
         soon as the task they are on is done. Java's shutdownNow also
         interrupts the running tasks; here that half is simply absent, and
         nothing pretends otherwise. */
      this.shutdown = true
      this.stopped = true
      while (!this.queue.isEmpty()) {
        dropped.add(this.queue.removeFirst())
      }
    }
    return dropped
  }

  public boolean awaitTermination(long millis) {
    long deadline = System.nanoTime() + millis * 1000000L
    while (true) {
      synchronized (this) {
        if (this.workers == 0) {
          return true
        }
      }
      long left = deadline - System.nanoTime()
      if (left <= 0) {
        return false
      }
      long slice = left / 1000000L
      if (slice > Concurrent.POLL_MILLIS) {
        slice = Concurrent.POLL_MILLIS
      }
      if (slice <= 0) {
        slice = 1
      }
      Thread.sleep(slice)
    }
  }

  public boolean isShutdown() {
    synchronized (this) {
      return this.shutdown
    }
  }

  public boolean isTerminated() {
    synchronized (this) {
      return this.shutdown && this.workers == 0
    }
  }

  /* The next task for this worker, or null when the pool is finished with it.
     The worker is idle from the moment it finds the queue empty here until the
     moment it takes a task, and it sleeps between the two -- a look at the
     queue under this monitor, a sleep outside it, and a look again. */
  private Runnable claim() {
    /* When this stretch of idleness started, for the keep-alive below. One call
       of claim is one stretch: it ends when a task is taken. */
    long idleSince = System.nanoTime()
    synchronized (this) {
      this.idle = this.idle + 1
    }
    while (true) {
      Runnable task = null
      boolean leaving = false
      synchronized (this) {
        if (!this.queue.isEmpty()) {
          this.idle = this.idle - 1
          task = this.queue.removeFirst()
        } else if (this.shutdown) {
          /* Nothing left to take and no more coming: this is where a worker
             normally leaves. The queue is the only thing that keeps a worker
             past shutdown, which is what makes "shutdown waits for the tasks
             that were already accepted" true. */
          this.idle = this.idle - 1
          this.workers = this.workers - 1
          leaving = true
        } else if (this.keepAlive > 0 &&
                   (System.nanoTime() - idleSince) / 1000000L >= this.keepAlive) {
          /* Idle for as long as this pool keeps anyone, and no work to take:
             the worker leaves. A cached pool is the only pool with a keep-alive
             above zero, and this is what makes it shrink back to no threads at
             all once the work stops. Nothing is lost by leaving: the queue was
             empty in this same critical section, and execute() starts a worker
             for whatever arrives after the idle count reaches zero. */
          this.idle = this.idle - 1
          this.workers = this.workers - 1
          leaving = true
        }
      }
      if (task != null) {
        return task
      }
      if (leaving) {
        return null
      }
      Thread.sleep(Concurrent.POLL_MILLIS)
    }
  }

  /* A worker died of a task's throwable, and this keeps the pool the size it
     was. Called with no monitor held. */
  private void workerFailed() {
    synchronized (this) {
      this.workers = this.workers - 1
      if (!this.stopped) {
        this.startWorker()
      }
    }
  }
}

/* The pool factories java.util.concurrent.Executors has, and only those: the
   scheduled and work-stealing ones need machinery this standard library does
   not have. */
class Executors {
  /* For the thread names. Java starts at 1 and numbers pools within the
     process; so does this. */
  private static int pools = 0

  private Executors() {
  }

  /* Called by the three factories. Threads are named the way
     java.util.concurrent names them -- pool-N-thread-M -- so a thread dump
     reads the same. */
  private static ExecutorService newPool(int core, long keepAlive) {
    Executors.pools = Executors.pools + 1
    return new ThreadPool(core, keepAlive, "pool-" + Executors.pools + "-thread-")
  }

  /* A pool of exactly this many threads, with every one of them started before
     the constructor returns. A pool of zero or fewer is an
     IllegalArgumentException, as it is in Java. */
  public static ExecutorService newFixedThreadPool(int nThreads) {
    if (nThreads <= 0) {
      throw new IllegalArgumentException("pool size must be greater than zero: " + nThreads)
    }
    return Executors.newPool(nThreads, 0L)
  }

  /* A pool of exactly one thread, which is a fixed pool of one and nothing
     more: Java's single-thread executor also wraps every task so that a later
     one cannot run before an earlier one has finished, and taking from one
     queue in order with one worker gives that here. */
  public static ExecutorService newSingleThreadExecutor() {
    return Executors.newPool(1, 0L)
  }

  /* The unbounded pool: one worker per task that arrives with none idle, each
     of them leaving after 60 seconds with nothing to do. */
  public static ExecutorService newCachedThreadPool() {
    return Executors.newPool(0, 60000L)
  }
}
